    Body = @'
  <p>At a florist counter, the customer sees flowers first and the wrap second. That sounds obvious until a large logo sticker takes over the bouquet. The person tying the stems has another concern: the sticker must land in one clean movement, while tissue shifts and the next order waits. I would design for both views.</p>
  <p>A bouquet photographed for a catalog is usually dry and carefully arranged. A real wrap may have damp stems, folded kraft, soft tissue and a ribbon pulling against the paper. The first useful sample is not a loose sticker. It is one finished bouquet being carried out of the shop.</p>
  <h2>Choose the Sticker's Job Before Its Shape</h2>
  <p>Is it a closure, a brand mark, a care reminder or a seasonal message? A round sticker across a fold may hold the paper. The same sticker on the front may only decorate. If a customer is expected to open the wrap cleanly, that difference matters.</p>
  <p>I would ask the florist to wrap the same style of bouquet twice, once with the normal paper and once with a seasonal alternative. Photograph both finished folds. Mark the area that stays flat after the ribbon is tied. That is the place to start measuring, not the center of a flat paper sheet.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Placement</th><th>Buyer decision</th><th>Trial</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Front wrap</td><td>Brand recognition</td><td>View a carried bouquet at normal distance</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Back fold</td><td>Closure or decoration</td><td>Carry and open the finished wrap</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tissue layer</td><td>Gentle opening</td><td>Peel from the actual tissue</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Care card</td><td>Useful instructions</td><td>Read after packing and delivery</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Seasonal design</td><td>Version count</td><td>Sort designs at the counter</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <h2>Water Exposure Is Not One Question</h2>
  <p>Moisture near stems, water mist on petals and rain during delivery do not touch the sticker in the same way. A label on the outside of a dry kraft sleeve has a different job from one near a damp inner wrap. I would describe the intended position and test it there.</p>
  <p>Paper, film and finish options may be discussed, but I would not call a whole bouquet wrap 'waterproof' because one sample survived a splash. The final paper, adhesive, application pressure and time before delivery all belong in a practical check. If the sticker is primarily decorative, avoid buying unnecessary protection at the cost of the visual feel the florist chose.</p>
  <h2>Use Original Botanical Artwork With Restraint</h2>
  <p>Line drawings of leaves, loose flowers, a vase silhouette or a small shop monogram can make a printed sticker feel like part of the bouquet. Blank white seals do not do that job. The artwork does not need to imitate a famous florist to look considered.</p>
  <p>I would keep the core mark stable and let seasonal color or a small floral motif change. A shop packing roses, mixed stems and dried flowers can still look like one shop. Review each version at the final printed size; thin script that reads on a screen may disappear on textured kraft.</p>
  <h2>Count What the Team Will Actually Apply</h2>
  <p>One bouquet may use a front brand sticker and a separate closure. If there are gift cards, care cards or delivery sleeves, the count changes again. Quote the placements, not only the number of bouquets. For example, 500 bouquets with two stickers each means 1,000 applications before any agreed spare allowance. That is a planning calculation, not a supplier policy.</p>
  <p>Sheet supply can help when a florist alternates seasonal designs. A roll may suit one repeated mark at a busy counter. The best format is the one the team can reach and peel without letting a bouquet wait. Orientation and separation matter more than a glossy mockup suggests.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send photographs of wrapped bouquets, not empty paper</li><li>Identify every sticker placement and its job</li><li>Measure the usable flat area in mm</li><li>Test opening on kraft and tissue separately</li><li>Show where damp stems and spray water reach</li><li>Approve each seasonal artwork version</li><li>Specify sheets, rolls or counted sets</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/products/custom-gift-stickers/">gift sticker category</a> shows related directions, while our <a href="/blog/roll-labels-vs-sheet-stickers/">roll-versus-sheet guide</a> helps choose a packing format. Send the finished-wrap photo through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote page</a>. If the person at the counter can apply the sticker without thinking about it, the design is doing useful work.</p>
