    Body = @'
  <p>At a cafe counter, the sticker has a few seconds to earn its place. A barista finishes the cup, adds a lid, calls an order and hands it over. The customer wants the drink they paid for; the owner wants the cup to look like the cafe. I would start with that hand-off instead of a perfect product photograph.</p>
  <p>A logo placed low on a cup can disappear under the sleeve. Placed high, it can interfere with a lid or the place where staff write the drink. One sticker cannot solve every position by being larger. The final cup, lid and sleeve need to be present at the artwork review.</p>
  <h2>Decide Whether the Sticker Brands, Identifies or Seals</h2>
  <p>A brand sticker gives the takeaway cup a visual identity. An order sticker identifies the drink or milk option. A seal across a delivery lid has a different opening job. These may share artwork, but I would quote them as separate placements unless a trial shows one piece truly does both.</p>
  <p>Take one hot cup, one iced cup and a delivery cup if all three matter. Fit the lid and sleeve, then mark each usable area in mm. Ask staff where their fingers land and where they read order notes. The positions can differ without making the brand inconsistent.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pack</th><th>Likely condition</th><th>What to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Hot paper cup</td><td>Warm wall and sleeve</td><td>Placement and visibility after grip</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Iced cup</td><td>Condensation</td><td>Adhesion and print after moisture</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Delivery lid</td><td>Opening and transport</td><td>Seal behavior on the exact lid</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Order label</td><td>Fast changing information</td><td>Writing or printing workflow</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Retail sleeve</td><td>Repeated handling</td><td>Artwork visibility and scuffing</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <h2>Heat and Condensation Need Separate Answers</h2>
  <p>A warm cup and a cold cup with water running down its side are not interchangeable label tests. I would describe the expected temperature and contact duration, then trial the proposed label on each real container. Even the same cup artwork can require different construction or placement.</p>
  <p>A sleeve can provide a convenient, drier surface for the brand mark. But if sleeves are optional, the cup still needs to make sense without one. If the mark is a delivery seal, ask how customers will open it and whether a torn lid or lifted edge is acceptable. Avoid calling a decorative printed circle a security feature without a specific tested structure.</p>
  <h2>Use Cafe Artwork That Helps Recognition</h2>
  <p>Original coffee bean drawings, a small steaming-cup icon, a neighborhood line sketch or a distinct cafe monogram can make the cup memorable. This is ordinary brand work, not a reason to copy a famous chain and not a reason to leave the sticker blank.</p>
  <p>I would keep the main mark bold enough to read while someone walks away with the cup. Save fine details for a larger sleeve or bag label. If different roast or drink families need cues, use a controlled color or icon system. The barista should not have to inspect decorative flourishes to identify an order.</p>
  <h2>Choose a Supply Format for the Counter</h2>
  <p>A roll can be quick for one repeated mark. Sheets can keep multiple drink options organized. The choice depends on counter layout, peel access, hand orientation and how many designs staff alternate between. I would ask two staff members to apply the sample during a normal rush, then listen to what slows them down.</p>
  <p>Count cups by placement. If a takeaway order uses one cup mark and one closure seal, 1,000 drinks imply 2,000 applications before spares in that planning example. The real mix of hot, iced and delivery orders matters more than a round total. Quote each design and format separately.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send the final hot and cold cups with lids and sleeves</li><li>Photograph each intended sticker position</li><li>Measure usable space in mm</li><li>Define heat, moisture and handling trials</li><li>Test the order-identification workflow</li><li>Choose rolls or sheets with staff input</li><li>Approve quantity by cup type and placement</li></ul>
  <p>Our <a href="/blog/roll-labels-vs-sheet-stickers/">roll and sheet comparison</a> helps with format; the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface guide</a> helps frame the cup trial. Use the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a> before requesting a quote. The result should still work after the sleeve goes on and the cup leaves the counter.</p>
